    "created_at": "2016-02-03T19:49:55Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-02-03T19:49:55Z",
    "body": "> the fastSeek() in the spec which has not been implemented AFAIK\n\n[Firefox 31 implemented fastSeek](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=778077), primarily for the benefit of touch controls on our mobile products.\n\nOur experience with fastSeek() has not been good.\n\nWe've had numerous bugs filed to the effect of \"I seeked and the seek didn't go where I expected\". This is particularly bad in files with dumb encodings like only a single keyframe. It's also bad because the location of sync-points is not exposed to JS.\n\nI'm planning on adding telemetry to track how often fastSeek gets used in Firefox and using that telemetry, along with feedback from other implementers as to whether they intend to implement fastSeek, to determine whether we remove our fastSeek implementation.\n\nWere fastSeek to remain in some fashion, I'd like it to be renamed to something other than \"fast\". People see \"fast\" and they think \"OMG I love fast! Why would I want slow??\". We should name it something that implies the trade-off being made. Like a \"mode:sloppy\", or \"mode: prev-sync-point\", in the SeekOptions dict proposed above.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
